0.4 g BT /F3 10 Tf 72 720 Td (A short explainer) Tj ET
0 g BT /F2 26 Tf 72 690 Td (The Broad Street Pump) Tj ET
0.3 g BT /F1 16 Tf 72 660 Td (London, 1854) Tj ET
0 g BT /F2 15 Tf 72 616 Td (What happened) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 592 Td (At the end of August 1854, cholera broke out in Soho, a crowded district of central) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 576.05 Td (London. Within ten days more than five hundred people who lived within a few streets) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 560.1 Td (of one another had died. Families fled; whole houses stood empty.) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 536.15 Td (Most doctors of the time believed that cholera spread through miasma, a poisonous) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 520.2 Td ('bad air' rising from filth and rotting matter. If that were true, the deaths should) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 504.25 Td (follow the smells: the drains, the cesspools, the tanneries.) Tj ET
0 g BT /F2 15 Tf 72 476.3 Td (A different idea) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 452.3 Td (John Snow, a London physician, thought otherwise. He had argued for years that) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 436.35 Td (cholera was carried in water contaminated by the waste of the sick, and that people) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 420.4 Td (caught it by swallowing it. Soho gave him a test.) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 396.45 Td (Snow went from house to house, writing down where each person who died had lived and) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 380.5 Td (where the household drew its water. In 1854 most homes had no tap: people carried) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 364.55 Td (water from public street pumps.) Tj ET
0 g BT /F2 15 Tf 72 336.6 Td (The question) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 312.6 Td (If the cause was the air, deaths should be spread over the whole smelly district. If) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 296.65 Td (the cause was the water, they should gather around one source. Page 2 shows what Snow) Tj ET
0 g BT /F1 11 Tf 72 280.7 Td (found.) Tj ET
0.45 g BT /F1 8.5 Tf 72 40 Td (The Broad Street Pump \(London, 1854\)  -  page 1 of 3) Tj ET
